 "8484b02a-7501-4030-ab16-cc0b64b9de46", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparison of translations"}], "description": "Students learn to slow down and ask literary, historical, and interpretive questions of primary texts."}]}, {"id": "bcebe7fa-aea9-416d-a456-a74079abc79b", "type": "topic", "title": "Canon, Textual History, Manuscripts, and Translation", "description": "Introduces Tanakh, Hebrew Bible, Old Testament, textual witnesses, and the stakes of translation.", "children": [{"id": "cfc7cce5-8a37-4df2-a263-c3c0576df821", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Canon formation", "children": [{"id": "080a9488-773e-4940-a39d-08bd0b349e3d", "type": "concept", "title": "Torah, Prophets, Writings"}, {"id": "ff0c4389-138a-4ed9-bcbd-b2b2deac08c9", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon boundaries"}, {"id": "6154ae9c-c8a9-46b7-a566-b87a42b1053b", "type": "concept", "title": "Book order and theology"}], "description": "Students examine how collections become authoritative and how order shapes interpretation."}, {"id": "d1ba8fb6-bc17-4770-abdf-a86612e60d35", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Textual witnesses", "children": [{"id": "767ef59c-e186-4581-9b13-6e50c8de76ac", "type": "concept", "title": "Masoretic Text"}, {"id": "f254e976-3e8a-4b36-8106-f14c9ec31261", "type": "concept", "title": "Dead Sea Scrolls"}, {"id": "a498c123-06b4-41dd-9a25-59ed0c488992", "type": "concept", "title": "Septuagint and Samaritan Pentateuch"}], "description": "The gist is that the Hebrew Bible has a textual history, not just a single timeless form."}, {"id": "c551c769-5bb1-468b-af66-043dc39fdec8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Translation choices", "children": [{"id": "cb4acf0b-e32f-4415-99a9-78a9cc48f4c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Formal and dynamic translation"}, {"id": "2461981b-5bc0-4160-9616-07f61474affa", "type": "concept", "title": "Key Hebrew terms"}, {"id": "daa2b3a1-342a-483f-908e-8e9f613ce9ca", "type": "concept", "title": "Footnotes and textual variants"}], "description": "Students learn that translations are interpretations that shape theology, tone, and meaning."}]}, {"id": "bcf0fa0b-bc4a-4a6c-b5c1-8135666e0436", "type": "topic", "title": "Ancient Near Eastern Context, Geography, Archaeology, and History", "description": "Places biblical literature in the worlds of Egypt, Mesopotamia, Canaan, Israel, Judah, empire, land, and material culture.", "children": [{"id": "26d7d3c7-6e20-4f1b-ad34-d5cbc23a747a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regional worlds", "children": [{"id": "9bc2b06b-228a-4b6b-a4c1-dd2b1f5dcfe2", "type": "concept", "title": "Egypt and Mesopotamia"}, {"id": "1c32c4c5-3b65-4631-8490-c7fd4d8c5c1a", "type": "concept", "title": "Canaan and the Levant"}, {"id": "142a5c63-a5d5-4404-8d41-5bf9c5eda4e7", "type": "concept", "title": "Assyria, Babylon, and Persia"}], "description": "Students place Israel and Judah within the wider eastern Mediterranean and Near Eastern environment."}, {"id": "6e64e63b-5347-4ab5-b739-507e2fc96f7c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Land and geography", "children": [{"id": "eaaac06e-10a6-47c2-9b6a-9e087b7a9558", "type": "concept", "title": "Wilderness, highlands, coast, and valleys"}, {"id": "46e71b9c-1def-461f-8def-b8af10d07314", "type": "concept", "title": "Jerusalem and temple geography"}, {"id": "26debfd6-3833-421a-b434-6478d6b541c4", "type": "concept", "title": "Borders and imagined land"}], "description": "The course links narrative, law, prophecy, and poetry to place."}, {"id": "8788d025-9ab0-4fdf-a72d-b5ad63655092", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Archaeology and evidence", "children": [{"id": "4a240bf4-6d52-4db5-9ed0-f18d9503af8a", "type": "concept", "title": "Inscriptions and seals"}, {"id": "16df1b37-60be-421b-a15d-b822fd689d30", "type": "concept", "title": "Settlement patterns"}, {"id": "e3de49e2-a681-442b-b960-749707eb1058", "type": "concept", "title": "Limits of archaeological correlation"}], "description": "Students learn what material evidence can and cannot prove about biblical texts."}]}, {"id": "8f9182f3-0d3d-4bf6-9f7a-872c9b711e7b", "type": "topic", "title": "Methods of Interpretation: Historical, Literary, Source, Redaction, and Reception Criticism", "description": "Builds the toolkit students need to read texts critically and compare scholarly arguments.", "children": [{"id": "c4f1c370-c3de-4463-a31d-92d4bcaf6a02", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historical-critical methods", "children": [{"id": "170e4759-8b4f-4e90-8f46-bc7efb6c742b", "type": "concept", "title": "Source criticism"}, {"id": "e887a29c-f734-4689-88b6-bd43cc32409d", "type": "concept", "title": "Form and tradition criticism"}, {"id": "39932d01-196f-408c-a3c0-f955587f0a94", "type": "concept", "title": "Redaction criticism"}], "description": "Students learn why scholars ask about sources, authorship, dating, editing, and historical setting."}, {"id": "42c15f12-5e4a-4cab-bbbe-e9b412b1f7e5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Literary methods", "children": [{"id": "1ea84b63-80da-4f92-912a-ca379a23d657", "type": "concept", "title": "Plot and characterization"}, {"id": "6cb1a8bd-d420-4fbb-8a2e-86d409910fa5", "type": "concept", "title": "Repetition and type-scenes"}, {"id": "59b59935-2094-4184-bd2c-2da60d6cb978", "type": "concept", "title": "Poetic parallelism"}], "description": "The point is to read biblical texts as crafted narratives and poems with structure, voice, and ambiguity."}, {"id": "f7cf84fe-0d3a-43a1-a5b3-f445274f97dd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reception and critical approaches", "children": [{"id": "e2e807fd-4ce9-42f8-816e-75a1dca281be", "type": "concept", "title": "Feminist and gender criticism"}, {"id": "16b419a0-6502-4705-8804-f65fb4bd73ae", "type": "concept", "title": "Postcolonial critique"}, {"id": "ab3112ee-40ad-4803-b42c-5d6bad72412d", "type": "concept", "title": "Jewish, Christian, and secular reception"}], "description": "Students see how texts generate later interpretations and how modern questions reshape readings."}]}, {"id": "fc7532b3-082e-4aee-abaa-a38949756f7d", "type": "topic", "title": "Creation, Primeval History, Myth, Genealogy, and Human Origins", "description": "Reads Genesis 1-11 as theological, literary, and cultural reflection on creation, violence, kinship, and human limits.", "children": [{"id": "ab8875de-3dc4-4586-8b28-de82a14017bf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Genesis creation accounts", "children": [{"id": "55484922-9afb-4595-9ed0-e3267c604b50", "type": "concept", "title": "Genesis 1 and Genesis 2-3"}, {"id": "2203651d-3803-40c6-8726-02122101e81e", "type": "concept", "title": "Image of God"}, {"id": "6bf5cef1-468f-4a37-8947-714b797799ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Sabbath and creation order"}], "description": "Students compare creation traditions as theological and literary claims about order, humanity, and God."}, {"id": "ef96b8e4-3d42-48b4-af5c-d1f9967e12bf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Primeval narratives", "children": [{"id": "506c09e3-896b-4b04-9ff4-47e06bfc7188", "type": "concept", "title": "Cain and Abel"}, {"id": "028c1a01-6d85-4644-9271-acf8967b2d6e", "type": "concept", "title": "Flood traditions"}, {"id": "880e328a-1c23-40ff-9025-1ec92207a493", "type": "concept", "title": "Tower of Babel"}], "description": "The course reads early Genesis as reflection on violence, limits, and divine-human relations."}, {"id": "f6891fff-baf5-4cb0-8469-fe238a0d81d0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Myth and genealogy", "children": [{"id": "98ab9a72-d503-4592-a2e7-2f1b9a821783", "type": "concept", "title": "Ancient Near Eastern parallels"}, {"id": "779332bf-df53-44eb-9b5d-b1b5ba26d863", "type": "concept", "title": "Genealogies and identity"}, {"id": "e8893470-82a7-4f31-b728-5ccdf7619148", "type": "concept", "title": "Etiology and cultural memory"}], "description": "Students learn to use 'myth' analytically rather than dismissively."}]}, {"id": "cf74e878-7555-4510-875e-965485f3b605", "type": "topic", "title": "Ancestors, Matriarchs and Patriarchs, Covenant, Promise, and Identity", "description": "Studies Genesis 12-50 as family narrative, 